 &lt;span&gt;Decision Architecture as Battlespace: Cognitive Warfare and the Defense of Institutional Meaning-Making&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;"], "abstract": "<jats:p>&lt;div&gt;
 Cognitive warfare is not primarily about the injection of false information. It is about shaping how institutions construct meaning. This paper reframes cognitive warfare as a contest over decision architecture: the systems through which institutions convert signals into interpretation, interpretation into convergence, and convergence into coordinated action. Building on scholarship in cognitive diversity, bounded rationality, organizational sensemaking, and institutional adaptation, it introduces the Decision Architecture Defense Model (DADM™) as a unified framework for analyzing institutional vulnerability and resilience under adversarial conditions.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 The DADM™ integrates three elements: a foundational cognitive formation layer, a cross-cutting institutional constraint field, and a five-layer decision architecture composed of Signal, Interpretation, Convergence, Decision, and Revision. The cognitive formation layer is grounded in research on professional socialization and cognitive bias rather than in any single theoretical tradition; the key point is that cognition arrives at institutional systems already shaped, before organizational norms have had an opportunity to operate.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 The Cognitive Warfare Vulnerability Assessment (CWVA™) operationalizes the DADM™ as a diagnostic instrument for evaluating institutional susceptibility to adversarial manipulation across seven dimensions. The Adaptive Cognitive Defense System (ACDS™) extends both frameworks into an operational architecture for hybrid human-machine decision environments, with four defined functional requirements: convergence-velocity monitoring, interpretive-diversity preservation, decision traceability, and designed revision triggers.
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Two historical cases, the Iraq WMD assessment (2002–2003) and the early global response to COVID-19 (2019–2020), are analyzed in depth through the DADM™ framework. Both reveal the same structural failure: the Convergence–Revision Gap, a condition in which interpretive closure outpaces the system’s capacity for correction. The central finding: institutional outcomes are determined less by data availability than by the architecture through which meaning is constructed. In cognitive warfare, the decisive terrain is not information. It is the system that transforms information into actionable reality.
